7592186045417] [17592186421817 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421817 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9035e657-b711-5639-a608-83ef4fb511b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421818 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421818 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421818 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421818 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b5729148-37a9-5323-892c-ea554a3ec7c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421819 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421819 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421819 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421819 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+4y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421819 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d0ba009-0a0e-534d-98fc-35f0419611d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421819 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{-2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{-2}-2u{x}^{-3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{x}^{-3}+6u{x}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{x}^{-3}+6u{x}^{-4})+5x({u}^{′}{x}^{-2}-2u{x}^{-3})+4u{x}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}{x}^{-1}+6u{x}^{-2}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{-1}-10u{x}^{-2}+4u{x}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}+{u}^{′}{x}^{-1} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{-2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={x}^{-5}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421820 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421820 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421820 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421820 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0,$ where $x>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nIII. The general solution of the equation is $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421820 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70cf3885-2e2c-5971-b49c-1988c3e0bb2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421820 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}})+2x(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})-2(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(12{x}^{2})+2x(4{x}^{3})-2{x}^{4}=18{x}^{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution but ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$ is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421821 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421821 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421821 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421821 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nIII. The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421821 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fd65812-f6ae-584d-bfe8-d11797aed930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421821 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =(1+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =(2+x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n(2+x){e}^{x}-4(x{e}^{x})=(2-3x){e}^{x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is false.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n4{e}^{2x}-4{e}^{2x}=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution while ${y}_{2}$ is a solution of the equation, hence $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$ is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421822 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421822 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421822 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421822 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421822 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab6bb605-4b84-5240-9d01-8856c6e45ebb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421822 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-3x}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9u{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{″}{e}^{-3x}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9u{e}^{-3x}-9u{e}^{-3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3x}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}+9u-9u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3x}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}-6w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{6x}\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}{e}^{6x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{-3x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}(\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}{e}^{6x}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=6$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{6x}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=6\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
